  "output_text": "Introduction/Context\n- The study applied proteomics-based non-negative matrix factorization (NMF) to IPMNs and PDACs to dissect tumor heterogeneity and progression features. Three NMF clusters emerged: NMF1 comprised entirely of PDACs; NMF2 contained both PDACs and IPMNs; NMF3 was largely IPMNs with a single PDAC. About 75% of IPMNs with an associated PDAC mapped to NMF2, suggesting this cluster may reflect a transitional/progression state. Feature genes used to define each NMF cluster and their pairwise contrasts were analyzed to interpret functional distinctions and potential clinical implications.\n\nKeywords\n- IPMN, IPMNs, IPN, IOOPN, ITPN, PDAC, proteomics, glycoproteomics, NMF, tumor heterogeneity, progression, cyst fluid, immunoglobulin genes, B cell signaling, metabolism, aldehyde metabolism, cytoskeleton remodeling, lipid transport.\n\nCandidate Genes\n- IGKV1D-17\n- APOM\n- AKR1B15\n- VWA8\n- SH3PXD2B\n- PGP\n\nPathway Enrichments\n- Note: The data provided includes DEG lists that inform pairwise differences between clusters (NMF1 vs NMF2, NMF3 vs NMF1, NMF3 vs NMF2). Direct enrichment tables (counts_by_database outputs, complete KEGG/GO term tables) are not available in the supplied files, so explicit per-database term counts or a “COMPLETE” top-term table cannot be generated here. The following targeted interpretation uses the DEG-derived gene sets to infer functional themes across cluster contrasts.\n\nTargeted (keyword-guided) subset\n- Immune/b-cell signature\n  - Genes linked: IGKV1D-17 (up in NMF2 vs 1), IGHG3, IGHM, HLA-DRA, ICAM1, ISG15 (from NMF1 feature list)\n  - Inference: NMF2 shows a humoral/immune signature consistent with B cell–associated activity in the transitional state between IPMN and PDAC.\n- Lipid transport/metabolism\n  - Gene: APOM (up in NMF2 vs 1)\n  - Inference: Elevated lipid-transport proteins in the transitional cluster may reflect altered cyst fluid lipoprotein dynamics or metabolic remodeling during progression.\n- Aldehyde/metabolic detoxification\n  - Gene: AKR1B15 (up in NMF3 vs 1 and NMF3 vs 2)\n  - Inference: Upregulation of aldehyde/metabolic detox pathways in NMF3 points to metabolic reprogramming characteristic of IPMN-dominant tumors advancing toward PDAC features.\n- Mitochondrial/metabolic remodeling\n  - Gene: VWA8 (up in NMF3 vs 1)\n  - Inference: Potential shifts in mitochondrial function or energy metabolism accompanying progression.\n- Cytoskeleton remodeling/cell migration\n  - Gene: SH3PXD2B (up in NMF1 vs 2)\n  - Inference: Enhanced cytoskeletal remodeling and migratory capacity in the PDAC-dominant NMF1, consistent with invasive potential.\n- Additional notes\n  - PGP and ARHGAP10 appear in pairwise contrasts, supporting themes of migration (cytoskeleton/cell signaling) and broader cytoskeletal regulation, respectively.\n\nKey Genes/Proteins (leading edge)\n- Up\n  | Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n  | IGKV1D-17 | Up (NMF2 vs 1) | Immunoglobulin light chain gene; B cell receptor signaling | Humoral immune response; B cell receptor signaling |\n  | APOM | Up (NMF2 vs 1) | Apolipoprotein M; lipid transport | Lipid transport; HDL metabolism |\n  | AKR1B15 | Up (NMF3 vs 1; NMF3 vs 2) | Aldo-keto reductase; aldehyde metabolism | Aldehyde metabolism; xenobiotic/metabolic detoxification |\n  | VWA8 | Up (NMF3 vs 1) | VWA domain-containing protein; potential mitochondrial function | Mitochondrial function; energy metabolism |\n  | SH3PXD2B | Up (NMF1 vs 2) | SH3 and PX domain-containing; cytoskeleton/cell migration | Cytoskeleton remodeling; cell motility/invadopodia |\n\n- Down\n  | Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n  | Not observed | Down | Not observed in leading-edge DEG set among the available comparisons | Not applicable |\n\nComparisons\n- Cluster-specific expression dynamics (pairwise contrasts):\n  - NMF2 vs NMF1: IGKV1D-17 and APOM are up in NMF2, suggesting humoral immune features and lipid-transport changes in the transitional (PDAC-associated) cluster.\n  - NMF3 vs NMF1: VWA8 and AKR1B15 are up in NMF3, indicating metabolic reprogramming and mitochondrial-associated changes in IPMN-dominant lesions with progression potential.\n  - NMF1 vs NMF2: SH3PXD2B (and PGP) are up in NMF1, highlighting cytoskeletal remodeling and migratory capacity more pronounced in the PDAC-pure cluster.\n- Overlaps with tissue context:\n  - NMF2 acts as a transition state clustering IPMN-associated PDACs, evidenced by enrichment of immunoglobulin-related genes, consistent with an immune-influenced transitional phenotype.\n  - NMF3 shows metabolic and mitochondrial signatures consistent with IPMN subsets undergoing metabolic shifts that may herald progression.\n  - NMF1 reflects strong cytoskeleton/motility signatures aligned with PDAC-intrinsic invasive biology.\n- Confidence considerations:\n  - The Up gene signatures come from DEG comparisons across pairwise cluster contrasts; Down signatures could not be robustly identified in the provided DEG tables, so cross-validation with additional contrasts or independent cohorts would strengthen confidence for Down-directed interpretations.\n\nImplications\n- Clinical relevance:\n  - NMF2’s humoral/immune gene pattern suggests a distinctive microenvironmental state that may inform risk stratification for IPMNs with PDAC progression potential and could influence surveillance strategies.\n  - NMF3’s metabolic/redox-linked signature (AKR1B15, VWA8) points to metabolic vulnerabilities that could be exploited therapeutically in IPMN lesions on a progression trajectory.\n  - NMF1’s cytoskeleton-related programs (SH3PXD2B) align with invasive/migratory capacities typical of PDAC, potentially linking this cluster to more aggressive disease and treatment resistance profiles.\n- Mechanistic link to progression:\n  - The three-cluster architecture captures a plausible progression axis: metabolic remodeling (NMF3) precedes or accompanies immune/humoral engagement (NMF2), while cytoskeletal/invasive programs (NMF1) become dominant in PDAC-typical states.\n- Biomarker potential:\n  - Ig-related transcripts (IGKV1D-17) and APOM in cyst fluid or tissue could serve as early indicators of transition toward PDAC within IPMN cohorts.\n\nNext Steps & Clarification\n- Validation in independent IPMN/PDAC cohorts with matched proteomics data to confirm the cluster-specific signatures.\n- Targeted proteomics or immunohistochemistry in tissue and cyst fluid to verify Ig-related and lipid-transport signatures in NMF2, and metabolic markers in NMF3.\n- Correlate NMF cluster assignment with clinical outcomes (progression-free survival, time to invasive cancer) to assess prognostic utility.\n- Expand analyses to glycoproteomics to determine whether glycosylation patterns accompany the NMF-defined states, particularly for immune-related and adhesion/migration pathways.\n- Explicit targeted checks:\n  - Presence/absence of IGKV1D-17 and APOM in IPMN patients with PDAC progression (NMF2 signature).\n  - Presence/absence of AKR1B15 and VWA8 in IPMN lesions poised to progress (NMF3 signature).\n  - Validation of SH3PXD2B upregulation in PDAC-dominant IPMN samples (NMF1 signature).\n\nReferences\n- Not included here as no PMIDs were validated in the provided steps.",
